      "id": 1,
      "text": "Parker Street in Fairfield honors its founder, Jacob S. Parker, who bought 150 acres of riverfront land after the Civil War.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.moderncities.com/article/2026-jan-7-historical-facts-about-fairfield"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1873, Jacob Parker helped build one of Duval County’s first paved roads, the toll road that became today’s Talleyrand Avenue.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.moderncities.com/article/2026-jan-7-historical-facts-about-fairfield"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fairfield hosted Florida’s first state fairgrounds in 1876, complete with exhibition halls, a horse racing track, and riverboat visitors.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.moderncities.com/article/2026-jan-7-historical-facts-about-fairfield"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Although Fairfield ceased to be an independent town, its early development left a lasting imprint on Jacksonville’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.moderncities.com/article/2026-jan-7-historical-facts-about-fairfield"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s historic street grid is split by the Hart Bridge, Mathews Bridge, and the MLK Jr. Bridge over the St. Johns River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.moderncities.com/article/2026-jan-7-historical-facts-about-fairfield"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Fairfield sits at the junction of County Roads 225 and 316, just 3.25 miles west of Reddick in Marion County.",
